{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B009",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B022",
    "B029"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B009 remains my preferred achievement. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea made it possible to retain the advantages of a large neutral-atom ensemble without the trapping shift destroying clock accuracy. Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's defining experimental development are complementary and identifiable. More importantly, this is no longer a promising precision technique: independent laboratories have repeatedly achieved extraordinary performance, permitting quantitative comparisons and fundamental measurements. It is a mature advance in how atomic energy differences can be measured, not merely an engineering refinement.",
    "case_against": "The main reservation is attribution. Optical frequency metrology and the later improvements that set present performance involve a broad community, so the record must sharply distinguish the original lattice-clock architecture from subsequent technical advances. Some of its most visible uses, including a changed time standard and routine relativistic geodesy, are still developing and should not be the central basis for the award.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that B007 is unusually mature, compactly attributed, and has had exceptional durable technological consequences, while questioning whether it is a fundamental physics discovery rather than materials engineering.",
        "response": "That qualification is decisive for me. Nd-Fe-B magnets are an extraordinary materials achievement, but on the supplied record their strongest case is industrial and technological impact. B009 more directly changed a fundamental precision-measurement regime and has been tested across laboratories, while retaining a similarly clear two-person core."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll places B022 close behind B009, emphasizing the mature and widely deployed precision-measurement platform created by atom interferometry, but notes its broad foundational credit.",
        "response": "I agree that atom interferometry is a profound and durable alternative, and its measurement of gravity and rotations has great scientific elegance. Yet its early conceptual and experimental lineages are harder to delimit. The magic-wavelength clock principle offers a more sharply bounded achievement with especially direct, reproducible quantitative realization."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors B028 because fluctuation relations provide a general, experimentally tested structure for irreversibility, while recognizing that credit among Evans, Jarzynski, Crooks, and Seifert exceeds the available places.",
        "response": "The physical insight in B028 is very strong, and I retain it as an important contender in principle. But the unresolved allocation among several independent formulations is substantial. For this round, B009 combines comparable maturity with a clearer link between the original idea, experimental realization, and later independent confirmation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival priority supports limiting the lattice-clock award to Katori and Ye rather than recognizing other indispensable optical-metrology contributors.",
      "How narrowly the citation can define the lattice-clock invention without absorbing the wider optical-clock revolution."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
